-- title:
--   The Fermat-ring products of one HvdH step cost at most a quarter of T'(N)
-- statement:
--   Let $K \ge 10^4$ and $N \ge 2^{2^{8K}+1}$, and let $q = \mathrm{mkParams}(K,N)$ be the published parameter choice of the Harvey–van der Hoeven step. With $T'(m) = (m+1)(\mathrm{zsz}(m)+2)$,
--   $$4\cdot\bigl(3\cdot 2^{\mathrm{logOuter}(q)}\cdot T'(f_n(q)+1)\bigr) \le T'(N),$$
--   where $f_n(q) = 4\cdot 2^{e}\cdot P$ is the Fermat exponent (products are computed modulo $2^{f_n}+1$) and $2^{\mathrm{logOuter}}$ is the number of outer Fermat-ring products per transform. Thus the three batches of recursive Fermat-ring multiplications contribute at most $T'(N)/4$ to the recursion inequality.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** `mkParams`, `Params.fn`, `Params.logOuter` are from the published definition `Def_IntMul_HvdH_MachineRef`; `TT'` is defined in `IntMul_HvdH_M_Defs3`.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.Asym.fermat_part` of the Lean development NLogN/HAsym.lean (lines 305–320), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

